  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the logger fails open or fails closed on sensitive data. Once logs persist to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and a
  file on disk, a leak stops being a transient console line and becomes a stored artifact with a lifetime. The current
  code makes this concrete: [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m logs at [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m and the caller logs the whole result object at
  line 24 — both inside the scope holding the password.

  - [1mDeny-list[0m — the logger scrubs values whose keys match known-sensitive patterns ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m). Low
    friction, call sites pass whatever they want. Fails open: the day someone adds [38;5;153mapiKey[39m or [38;5;153mssn[39m and doesn't update the
    pattern list, it gets persisted, and nobody finds out until an audit.
  - [1mAllow-list[0m — structured context is dropped unless the field is explicitly declared loggable. Fails closed: a new
    field is invisible until someone deliberately permits it. Costs a little friction at every new call site, and that
    friction grows with the app.
  - [1mCall-site discipline only[0m — no redaction machinery, just a rule that you don't log secrets. Zero code. Depends
    entirely on every future change being careful, which is the assumption that produced the current [38;5;153mlogin[39m call.

  Cheap to change later: adding entries to either list. Expensive to change later: switching direction, because a
  deny-list codebase accumulates call sites passing whole objects, and tightening to an allow-list afterward means
  auditing all of them. Also expensive: logs already written under a leaky policy — you can change the code, not the
  data on someone's disk.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m allow-list. The usual argument against it is friction, and this app is four functions — the
  friction is close to zero right now, which is exactly when it's cheap to adopt and won't be later. And since you're
  persisting, a redaction miss has a much longer half-life than it would with transient console output.
